# LWS operations -> HTTP-in-RDF mapping rules
#
# Test steps are written as higher-level LWS operations
# (lwst:CreateResource, lwst:ReadResource, lwst:UpdateResource,
# lwst:DeleteResource -- cf. the operations in
# https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#operations).
# These rules map each operation to a basic HTTP request, described with
# the W3C HTTP Vocabulary in RDF (https://www.w3.org/TR/HTTP-in-RDF10/),
# plus the response an LWS server is expected to return by default.
#
# Usage: once a harness has substituted execution parameters
# (lwst:target templates resolved to a concrete http:absoluteURI), an N3
# reasoner run over the test data plus this rule set completes the
# request description of each step (method etc.) and adds
# lwst:expectedResponse -- the default response an LWS server is
# expected to return; a test may override it with lwst:expects.
#
# The rules deliberately use only Basic Graph Patterns so they run on
# the n3.js reasoner (https://www.npmjs.com/package/n3#Reasoning):
# * no built-ins (e.g. list:in), so header expectations are expressed
#   as individual http:headers statements rather than members of one
#   header list;
# * blank nodes in a conclusion are created once per rule, not per
#   firing -- so per-step request data (method, URI, ...) attaches to
#   the operation node itself: the operation doubles as the
#   HTTP-in-RDF request description. Expected responses carry content
#   that is constant per operation type, so they can safely share a
#   conclusion blank node.

@prefix http:     <http://www.w3.org/2011/http#> .
@prefix methods:  <http://www.w3.org/2011/http-methods#> .
@prefix headers:  <http://www.w3.org/2011/http-headers#> .
@prefix statuses: <http://www.w3.org/2011/http-statusCodes#> .
@prefix lwst:     <https://www.w3.org/ns/lws-tests/v1#> .
@prefix xsd:      <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# Read resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#read-resource
{
  ?op a lwst:ReadResource ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:methodName "GET" ;
      http:mthd methods:GET ;
      lwst:expectedResponse [
        a http:Response ;
        http:statusCodeValue "200"^^xsd:integer ;
        http:sc statuses:OK
      ] .
} .

# Create resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#create-resource
# POST to the target container; the server creates a new resource and
# responds 201 Created with a Location header pointing at it.
{
  ?op a lwst:CreateResource ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:methodName "POST" ;
      http:mthd methods:POST ;
      lwst:expectedResponse [
        a http:Response ;
        http:statusCodeValue "201"^^xsd:integer ;
        http:sc statuses:Created ;
        http:headers [
          a http:ResponseHeader ;
          http:fieldName "Location" ;
          http:hdrName headers:location
        ]
      ] .
} .

# Update resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#update-resource
# PUT (full replacement) binding. PATCH is an alternative binding for
# partial updates, left out until the LWS spec settles update semantics.
# Success status codes vary (200/204/205), so tests assert expectations
# explicitly via lwst:expects.
{
  ?op a lwst:UpdateResource ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:methodName "PUT" ;
      http:mthd methods:PUT .
} .

# Delete resource -- https://w3c.github.io/lws-protocol/lws10-core/#delete-resource
# LWS servers answer 200 or 204 on success, so no default status here;
# tests assert the expected status explicitly (cf. Test2).
{
  ?op a lwst:DeleteResource ;
      http:absoluteURI ?uri .
}
=>
{
  ?op a http:Request ;
      http:methodName "DELETE" ;
      http:mthd methods:DELETE .
} .
